Every commuter who switches from car to transit removes, on average, 4,800 pounds of CO₂ per year from the atmosphere — equivalent to planting 60 trees.
Singapore's Electronic Road Pricing system, combined with excellent public transit, has kept traffic congestion flat since the 1990s despite car ownership doubling.
Air pollution from traffic kills an estimated 310,000 Europeans per year; cities with high transit use (Amsterdam, Vienna, Zurich) have PM2.5 levels 30–40% lower than car-dominated equivalents.
